<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.13557v1/images_agent/explain.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:429/539;" width="429" height="539" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 3</span>: </span><span id="S3.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The implementation of CGRA design selection with LLM self-learning based on EDA tool report feedback.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.4" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Design Methods of MACO Framwork</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our design methodology for the four stages is as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage 1: CGRA Design –</span> CGRA co-designer generates candidate CGRA designs based on user input while iteratively refining designs using historical data, which leverages HW/SW co-design principles to produce a diverse set of architecture candidates. Each candidate encompasses parameters at both the HW/SW levels, including functional unit allocation and loop unrolling factors, ensuring that we can generate effiencicy CGRAs by covering a wide range of design trade-offs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To produce high-quality CGRA candidates, domain-specific knowledge is embedded into prompts, enabling the agent to effectively balance compilation strategies, mapping efficiency, and hardware architecture optimization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage 2: Design Verification and Correction –</span> This stage employs MapFixer to invoke simulation tools for validation. As automatically generated designs may contain syntax, compilation, or structural errors, designs that pass validation proceed directly to the next stage, while those that fail are automatically repaired using the LLM’s reasoning capabilities, guided by information from reports and logs. This process ensures that only valid and executable CGRA architectures advance to subsequent stages.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage 3: Multi-Judge Selection –</span> This stage employs a hierarchical, two-level filtering process. At the first level, the coarse-grained judge leverages the reasoning capabilities of LLM along with embedded optimization knowledge to rapidly identify candidates with high performance potential.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">At the second level, the fine-grained agent works alongside CGRA design tool and synthesis tool. In the early iterations, synthesis tools provide accurate power and area analysis, while open-source CGRA tool<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> estimate speedup, enabling the selection of the best-performing design. Meanwhile, the fine-grained judge also makes the best choice and learns from the reports if its selection is incorrect. As iterations progress, the fine-grained agent gradually learns from experimental data, evolving into an autonomous evaluator capable of replacing the tools to make the final CGRA design selection. This process leverages an adaptive-confidence, LLM self-learning approach for CGRA design selection, as outlined in Algorithm 1, which will be discussed in detail in the following sections.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage 4: Performance Evaluation and Feedback –</span> In stage 4, final designs that are not evaluated in Stage 3 undergo comprehensive performance and power assessment using design and synthesis tools. The results are stored as historical data, which are fed back to the agents in Stage 1 to further enhance the CGRA designer’s generation quality. Upon completion of the final iteration, the framework outputs the final design results.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS3.4" class="ltx_text">III-C</span> </span><span id="S3.SS3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Adaptive Confidence CGRA Design Selection with LLM Self-learning</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Stage 3, when selecting best design from the top K designs, we consider two approaches: evaluation via synthesis tools and inference via LLM reasoning(Fine-grained Judge). Considering the time-consuming nature of tool-based evaluation and the potential error rate of LLM reasoning, we propose an adaptive confidence CGRA selection method with LLM self-learning to balance accuracy and efficiency.</p>
</div>
