<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S3.T3.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:129%;">TABLE III</span>: </span><span id="S3.T3.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:129%;">Evaluation of CGRA design performance across different model sizes in MACO framework.<span id="S3.T3.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> The evaluation is based on a one-shot of our framework. Note: SR1: success rate before Stage 2, which represents the probability of successfully mapping the kernel on the CGRA design; SR2: success rate after Stage 2. Speedup is evaluated using the OpenCGRA toolflow<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, with an in-order CPU’s performance as the baseline, and power is estimated under the ASAP 7 nm PDK using Synopsys Design Compiler tool.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The specific algorithm is presented in Algorithm 1. Initially, a confidence measure and trust threshold are established. For each iteration, if LLM’s reasoning confidence falls below the threshold, EDA tools (e.g., simulations and synthesis) are used to evaluate the top-k candidate designs, from which the best design is selected. Simultaneously, the LLM engages in contrastive learning against the EDA tool reports and its own choices to enhance its capability. As iterations progress, the confidence score increases based on the consistency between LLM predictions and simulated results. Once the confidence surpasses the threshold, the system transitions to primarily relying on LLM-based reasoning, enabling rapid and accurate design selection. To ensure system stability, simulation-based evaluations are periodically invoked every N iterations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This approach fully leverages the model’s inference capabilities to enable rapid iterative optimization and selection of CGRA design. Compared with traditional reinforcement learning methods, it offers superior scalability, as it is not restricted to a single learning framework. During subsequent design evolution, when new software compilation parameters or hardware architecture parameters are introduced, the model can retain existing knowledge while continuously learning and integrating new information, progressively refining and enhancing its capabilities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Figure 3 illustrates how the fine-grained agent continuously learns and improves its CGRA design proficiency based on EDA tool reports and its own selection experiences.</p>
</div>
