<td id="S4.T4.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S4.T4.2.4.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">24</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S4.T4.2.4.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">16</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.2.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S4.T4.2.4.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">12</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.2.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S4.T4.2.4.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">10</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T4.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T4.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S4.T4.2.5.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Topology</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S4.T4.2.5.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Crossbar</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S4.T4.2.5.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Mesh</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.2.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S4.T4.2.5.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Kingmesh</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.2.5.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S4.T4.2.5.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">KingMesh</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T4.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T4.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S4.T4.2.6.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Unrolling factor</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S4.T4.2.6.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">2</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.2.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S4.T4.2.6.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">4</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.2.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S4.T4.2.6.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">2</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.2.6.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S4.T4.2.6.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">3</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T4.2.7" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T4.2.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S4.T4.2.7.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Vectorize factor</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.2.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S4.T4.2.7.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">1</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.2.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S4.T4.2.7.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">2</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.2.7.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S4.T4.2.7.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">1</span></td>
<td id="S4.T4.2.7.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S4.T4.2.7.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">2</span></td></tr>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T4.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:129%;">TABLE IV</span>: </span><span id="S4.T4.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:129%;">Comparison of HW/SW parameters across different CGRA generation methods.<span id="S4.T4.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
The listed architecture parameters are derived from CGRA designs produced by each method for the <span id="S4.T4.9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">spmv</span> kernel, with the objective of enhancing energy efficiency.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS2.4" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span> </span><span id="S4.SS2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Result and Analysis</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MACO framework based on different LLM models:</span> Table <a href="#S3.T3" title="TABLE III ‣ III-C Adaptive Confidence CGRA Design Selection with LLM Self-learning ‣ III MACO Methodology ‣ MACO: A Multi-Agent LLM-Based Hardware/Software Co-Design Framework for CGRAs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a> summarizes the one-shot results of MACO across different models. As is shown in Table <a href="#S3.T3" title="TABLE III ‣ III-C Adaptive Confidence CGRA Design Selection with LLM Self-learning ‣ III MACO Methodology ‣ MACO: A Multi-Agent LLM-Based Hardware/Software Co-Design Framework for CGRAs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a>, all models using MACO are able to generate valid architectures that can be successfully mapped. However, it is worth noting that the applicability of MACO improves as model capacity increases. Although the framework can effectively repair deficiencies in CGRAs generated by different models, the final architectures designed with larger models consistently achieve higher performance, indicating that model scale contributes positively to design quality.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MACO performance vs. other approaches:</span>
Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="TABLE II ‣ III-C Adaptive Confidence CGRA Design Selection with LLM Self-learning ‣ III MACO Methodology ‣ MACO: A Multi-Agent LLM-Based Hardware/Software Co-Design Framework for CGRAs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a> reports comparative results across different kernels with power consumption as the optimization objective. We evaluate MACO framework against other LLM-based methods, plus manual design. The results demonstrate that MACO consistently outperforms nearly all other approaches in power optimization (under a speedup guarantee). Furthermore, we extend the evaluation to power efficiency, as illustrated in Figure 4. In this setting, we compare MACO (co-design) with MACO (without software optimization) and with other methods. MACO’s co-design approach achieves superior power efficiency, highlighting the importance of incorporating HW/SW co-design in the CGRA design process.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In addition, we summarize the HW/SW parameters obtained by different CGRA design methods for the spmv kernel, targeting power efficiency optimization, as shown in Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="TABLE IV ‣ IV-A Experiment Setup ‣ IV Evaluation ‣ MACO: A Multi-Agent LLM-Based Hardware/Software Co-Design Framework for CGRAs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a>. It can be observed that MACO tends to select parameters that yield higher power efficiency, such as the kingmesh topology.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evolution of results over iterations in MACO:</span> To evaluate the iterative performance and convergence of MACO, we measure the power efficiency of three representative kernels (fft, spmv, and gemm) across iterations, as shown in Figure 5. The results demonstrate that with increasing iterations, the performance of the generated CGRAs is progressively improved until reaching convergence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Physical Implementation of MACO CGRAs:</span>
To further validate MACO’s effectiveness with an advanced technology node, we picked a domain-specific CGRA generated by MACO and implemented it with ASIC flow using ASAP 7nm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>, producing a complete GDSII of CGRA fabric from MACO-generated RTL.
Figure 6 presents the layout of the MACO-CGRA specialized for the embedded domain. The resulting 4×4 CGRA achieves a compact area of 2.99 mm<sup id="S4.SS2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_sup">2</sup> and demonstrates an average power consumption of 30.49 mW (post layout) under 0.7 V at 435 MHz, validating MACO generated CGRAs across the entire physical design flow.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.13557v1/images_agent/chip_layout.png" id="S4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:333/305;" width="333" height="305" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F6.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 6</span>: </span><span id="S4.F6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The physical layout of an embedded domain-specific CGRA generated by MACO with ASAP 7nm library.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">V </span><span id="S5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Conclusion</span></h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we present MACO, a multi-agent LLM-based framework for CGRA design. By decomposing the design process and performing iterative optimization, the framework effectively identifies optimal CGRA architectures for a diverse range of application kernels.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Experimental results on a comprehensive benchmark demonstrate that MACO can generate high-quality CGRA architectures with improved performance, efficiency, and resource utilization compared to traditional design approaches. To our knowledge, this is the first work to leverage LLMs in a structured multi-agent setting for CGRA design. Future work will explore extending the framework to support more complex architectures, integrate additional optimization objectives, and incorporate real hardware deployment feedback to further enhance design quality and practicality.</p>
</div>
</section>
